// Hand-written weakening ideal: a default-on grant may also be dropped when
// the other channel takes the bus, not only when its own request arrives.
//
// `mucs` returns { r2 -> F a2, !(a1 && a2), a1 W r1 } and, by symmetry, the
// same trio with the indices swapped. Both cores say the same thing. `a1 W r1`
// makes a1 on by default until the first r1, `a2 W r2` does the same for a2,
// and at time zero -- before either request has arrived -- the two demand a1
// and a2 together, which mutual exclusion forbids outright. Even later the
// deadlock persists: with r1 never raised, a1 is held forever, so a2 never
// rises and an r2 request goes unanswered.
//
// Weakening each `W` in its second argument (W is monotone there, so this is
// implied by the original) says the default grant is released either by its
// own request or by the other channel claiming the bus. That is the handoff
// the arbiter already performs -- a1 is idle-parked until someone wants the
// bus -- and it keeps both the acknowledgement liveness and the mutual
// exclusion intact, rather than paying for realizability with the safety
// property.
INFO {
  TITLE:       "Lily Demo V15"
  DESCRIPTION: "weakened: a default grant is released by a handoff too"
  SEMANTICS:   Mealy
  TARGET:      Mealy
}

MAIN {

  INPUTS {
    r1;
    r2;
  }

  OUTPUTS {
    a1;
    a2;
  }

  INVARIANTS {
    r1 -> F a1;
    r2 -> F a2;
    !(a1 && a2);
  }

  GUARANTEES {
    a1 W (r1 || a2);
    a2 W (r2 || a1);
  }

}
